{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "# Diverse Short Synthetic Time Series Dataset Demo\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook demonstrates the use of a programmatically generated synthetic time series dataset for evaluating micro-niche adaptive forecasting methods. It showcases how to load, process, and perform a basic 3-point moving average forecast against a naive last-value forecast. The dataset is designed to represent distinct 'micro-environmental cues' such as linear trends (upward/downward), flat periods, oscillatory patterns (varying frequencies/amplitudes), sudden step changes in value, and shifts in volatility. This collection is ideal for evaluating micro-niche adaptive forecasting methods due to its controlled diversity and short, focused examples."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "%%capture --no-display\n",
    "import subprocess, sys\n",
    "def _pip(*a): subprocess.check_call([sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', 'install', '-q', *a])\n",
    "\n",
    "# Packages NOT pre-installed on Colab (always install everywhere)\n",
    "_pip('loguru==0.7.2')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Core packages (pre-installed on Colab, install locally to match Colab env)\n",
    "if 'google.colab' not in sys.modules:\n",
    "    _pip('numpy==2.0.2', 'pandas==2.2.2', 'scikit-learn==1.6.1', 'matplotlib==3.10.0')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import json\n",
    "from pathlib import Path\n",
    "from loguru import logger\n",
    "import sys\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Suppress loguru's default handler to avoid duplicate output in notebooks\n",
    "logger.remove()\n",
    "logger.add(sys.stdout, level="INFO", format="{time:HH:mm:ss}|{level:<7}|{message}")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Data Loading Helper\n",
    "\n",
    "This cell defines a helper function to load the `mini_demo_data.json` dataset. It first attempts to load the data from a GitHub URL for Colab compatibility, and falls back to a local file if the URL is inaccessible. This ensures the notebook works both in Colab and in a local environment."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "GITHUB_DATA_URL = "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AMGrobelnik/ai-invention-1560d8-micro-niche-adaptive-forecasting-for-sho/main/round-2/dataset-1/demo/mini_demo_data.json"\n",
    "\n",
    "def load_data():\n",
    "    try:\n",
    "        import urllib.request\n",
    "        logger.info(f"Attempting to load data from GitHub URL: {GITHUB_DATA_URL}")\n",
    "        with urllib.request.urlopen(GITHUB_DATA_URL) as response:\n",
    "            return json.loads(response.read().decode())\n",
    "    except Exception as e:\n",
    "        logger.warning(f"Could not load data from GitHub URL: {GITHUB_DATA_URL}. Error: {e}. Attempting local fallback.")\n",
    "    if Path("mini_demo_data.json").exists():\n",
    "        logger.info("Loading data from local file: mini_demo_data.json")\n",
    "        with open("mini_demo_data.json", 'r') as f: return json.load(f)\n",
    "    raise FileNotFoundError("Could not load mini_demo_data.json from GitHub or local file.")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Load the Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "This cell loads the `mini_demo_data.json` using the helper function defined above. The dataset contains synthetic time series examples, each with an input window of past values and a corresponding next value to be predicted."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "data = load_data()\n",
    "logger.info(f"Loaded {len(data['datasets'])} datasets with a total of {sum(len(d['examples']) for d in data['datasets'])} examples.")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Configuration\n",
    "\n",
    "This cell defines tunable parameters for the forecasting models. For this demo, the input window size is fixed at 3, as specified by the problem statement for a 3-point moving average."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Tunable parameters\n",
    "MIN_INPUT_WINDOW_SIZE = 3 # Fixed for 3-point moving average"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Data Preprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "This section processes the loaded raw JSON data into a more usable pandas DataFrame format. It extracts the input sequences, actual output values, and relevant metadata for each example. The string representations of input arrays and output values are converted to numerical types."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "processed_examples = []\n",
    "for dataset_entry in data['datasets']:\n",
    "    dataset_name = dataset_entry['dataset']\n",
    "    for example in dataset_entry['examples']:\n",
    "        input_sequence = json.loads(example['input']) # Convert JSON string to list of floats\n",
    "        output_value = float(example['output'])       # Convert string to float\n",
    "        processed_examples.append({\n",
    "            'dataset': dataset_name,\n",
    "            'input': input_sequence,\n",
    "            'actual_output': output_value,\n",
    "            'series_id': example['metadata_series_id'],\n",
    "            'pattern_type': example['metadata_pattern_type'],\n",
    "            'forecasting_point_index': example['metadata_forecasting_point_index']\n",
    "        })\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.DataFrame(processed_examples)\n",
    "logger.info(f"Processed {len(df)} examples into a DataFrame.")\n",
    "display(df.head())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Forecasting Models\n",
    "\n",
    "This section implements two simple forecasting models:\n",
    "\n",
    "1.  **3-Point Moving Average**: Predicts the next value by taking the average of the last three input values.\n",
    "2.  **Naive Last-Value Forecast**: Predicts the next value by simply repeating the last observed value in the input sequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "These models are applied to each example in the dataset, and their predictions are added to the DataFrame."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def three_point_moving_average(input_seq):\n",
    "    if len(input_seq) < MIN_INPUT_WINDOW_SIZE:\n",
    "        return np.nan # Not enough data for a 3-point average\n",
    "    return np.mean(input_seq[-MIN_INPUT_WINDOW_SIZE:])\n",
    "\n",
    "def naive_last_value_forecast(input_seq):\n",
    "    if not input_seq:\n",
    "        return np.nan\n",
    "    return input_seq[-1]\n",
    "\n",
    "df['3_point_ma_prediction'] = df['input'].apply(three_point_moving_average)\n",
    "df['naive_prediction'] = df['input'].apply(naive_last_value_forecast)\n",
    "\n",
    "logger.info("Generated predictions using 3-point moving average and naive last-value forecasts.")\n",
    "display(df.head())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Evaluation\n",
    "\n",
    "The models are evaluated based on their Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) against the actual output values. These metrics quantify the accuracy of each forecasting method."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error\n",
    "\n",
    "results = []\n",
    "\n",
    "for forecast_col in ['3_point_ma_prediction', 'naive_prediction']:\n",
    "    # Drop rows where prediction is NaN due to insufficient input data\n",
    "    temp_df = df.dropna(subset=[forecast_col])\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if not temp_df.empty:\n",
    "        mae = mean_absolute_error(temp_df['actual_output'], temp_df[forecast_col])\n",
    "        rmse = np.sqrt(mean_squared_error(temp_df['actual_output'], temp_df[forecast_col]))\n",
    "        results.append({\n",
    "            'Model': forecast_col.replace('_prediction', '').replace('_', ' ').title(),\n",
    "            'MAE': mae,\n",
    "            'RMSE': rmse\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        logger.warning(f"No valid predictions for {forecast_col}. Skipping evaluation.")\n",
    "\n",
    "results_df = pd.DataFrame(results)\n",
    "logger.info("Evaluation complete.")\n",
    "display(results_df)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Visualization of Forecasts\n",
    "\n",
    "This section provides a visual comparison of the actual values, 3-point moving average predictions, and naive last-value predictions for each synthetic time series. This helps in qualitatively assessing the performance of the forecasting models across different series patterns."
   ],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "unique_series_ids = df['series_id'].unique()\n",
    "\n",
    "num_series = len(unique_series_ids)\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(num_series, 1, figsize=(12, 5 * num_series), sharex=False, sharey=False)\n",
    "fig.suptitle('Forecast Comparison for Diverse Synthetic Time Series', y=1.02, fontsize=16)\n",
    "\n",
    "if num_series == 1: # Handle case of single subplot\n",
    "    axes = [axes]\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, series_id in enumerate(unique_series_ids):\n",
    "    series_df = df[df['series_id'] == series_id].sort_values(by='forecasting_point_index')\n",
    "    ax = axes[i]\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Reconstruct the full series for plotting\n",
    "    # This assumes the first 'input' sequence represents the start of the series\n",
    "    # and 'actual_output' are subsequent points.\n",
    "    if not series_df.empty:\n",
    "        # Get the first input sequence\n",
    "        initial_sequence = series_df['input'].iloc[0]\n",
    "        # The actual outputs are the subsequent points starting from the end of the initial_sequence\n",
    "        full_series_values = initial_sequence + series_df['actual_output'].tolist()\n",
    "        \n",
    "        ax.plot(full_series_values, label='Full Series (Actual)', marker='o', linestyle='-')\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # Plot predictions at their respective forecasting points\n",
    "        # The x-axis index for prediction is 'forecasting_point_index'\n",
    "        ax.plot(series_df['forecasting_point_index'], series_df['3_point_ma_prediction'], label='3-Point MA Prediction', marker='x', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7)\n",
    "        ax.plot(series_df['forecasting_point_index'], series_df['naive_prediction'], label='Naive Prediction', marker='^', linestyle=':', alpha=0.7)\n",
    "\n",
    "    ax.set_title(f"Series ID: {series_id} (Pattern: {series_df['pattern_type'].iloc[0]})", fontsize=12)\n",
    "    ax.set_xlabel('Time Point')\n",
    "    ax.set_ylabel('Value')\n",
    "    ax.legend()\n",
    "    ax.grid(True)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  }
 ],
